WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.590 --> 00:00:04.990
Marvel readers have been familiar with Venom
for decades, but the half-alien anti-hero

00:00:04.990 --> 00:00:07.980
is only just now making his standalone big-screen
debut.

00:00:08.300 --> 00:00:11.680
From who's playing who, to the comics you
need to brush up on, here's everything you

00:00:11.680 --> 00:00:13.300
need to know before you see Venom.

00:00:14.360 --> 00:00:15.260
A symbiote story

00:00:16.120 --> 00:00:21.080
Also known as the Klyntars, the symbiotes
are a race of extraterrestrial, fully sentient

00:00:21.080 --> 00:00:21.580
parasites.

00:00:21.860 --> 00:00:26.300
They live by cocooning living organisms and
feeding off their adrenaline, sucking hosts

00:00:26.300 --> 00:00:27.920
dry before leaving them for dead.

00:00:28.270 --> 00:00:32.880
The Venom symbiote is unique in its species
for desiring long-lasting bonds over quick

00:00:32.900 --> 00:00:35.340
kills a trait which makes it an outcast.

00:00:35.580 --> 00:00:40.560
Making its first appearance in a string of
1984 comics, the Venom symbiote originally

00:00:40.560 --> 00:00:44.540
attached itself to Peter Parker in the form
of a black Spider-Man suit.

00:00:44.920 --> 00:00:48.739
While Peter initially enjoyed the benefits
of the symbiote, he worked to rid himself

00:00:48.739 --> 00:00:53.309
of it after he discovered its villainous nature,
eventually driving it away by exploiting its

00:00:53.309 --> 00:00:54.760
weakness to loud sounds.

00:00:55.600 --> 00:00:56.460
A new host

00:00:57.480 --> 00:01:02.720
The best-known human half of Venom is Eddie
Brock, played by Tom Hardy in the 2018 film.

00:01:02.920 --> 00:01:07.420
Created by David Michelinie and Todd McFarlane,
the character made his first full-fledged

00:01:07.420 --> 00:01:10.780
appearance in a 1988 issue of Amazing Spider-Man.

00:01:10.980 --> 00:01:16.240
In the comics, Brock is a once-respected reporter
who lost his job as an indirect result of

00:01:16.240 --> 00:01:17.440
Spider-Man's crime-fighting.

00:01:17.840 --> 00:01:22.940
Specifically, he's fired after a high-profile
story about a serial killer's true identity

00:01:22.940 --> 00:01:25.940
is discredited after Spider-Man nabs the real
culprit.

00:01:26.069 --> 00:01:31.000
Furious and seeking guidance, the Catholic
Brock takes refuge in a church the same church

00:01:31.000 --> 00:01:34.580
whose loud bells Peter successfully used to
separate from the symbiote.

00:01:34.780 --> 00:01:39.020
After they encounter each other, Brock and
the symbiote bond over a shared hatred of

00:01:39.020 --> 00:01:41.260
Spider-Man and Venom as we know him is born.

00:01:41.570 --> 00:01:47.040
Existing outside of the Marvel Cinematic Universe,
the Venom movie significantly tweaks Brock's

00:01:47.049 --> 00:01:51.159
origin story, removing the Peter Parker element
and setting the action entirely in Brock's

00:01:51.160 --> 00:01:52.520
home city of San Francisco.

00:01:52.909 --> 00:01:56.920
In the movie, Venom bonds with Brock without
ever encountering Peter Parker as you can

00:01:56.920 --> 00:01:59.940
tell from the lack of white spider markings
on the movie Venom's suit.

00:02:00.740 --> 00:02:01.960
Adapting the source

00:02:02.380 --> 00:02:07.439
While the Venom movie is an original story,
it's based on two distinct Marvel comic arcs.

00:02:07.439 --> 00:02:13.260
The first, 1993's Lethal Protector, is a six-issue
story that sees Spider-Man and Venom arranging

00:02:13.260 --> 00:02:16.600
a truce, with the latter agreeing to leave
New York for San Francisco.

00:02:16.920 --> 00:02:21.680
Venom takes up a role as a Bay Area antihero,
eventually teaming up with Spider-Man again

00:02:21.680 --> 00:02:24.250
to fend off five new villainous symbiotes.

00:02:24.250 --> 00:02:29.520
The second chunk of source material is 1995's
"Planet of the Symbiotes", a five-issue arc

00:02:29.520 --> 00:02:34.260
which also follows Venom and his allies as
they work to stop a worldwide symbiote invasion.

00:02:34.550 --> 00:02:39.260
Combine the two together, and you get the
movie's story of a brutal west coast superhero,

00:02:39.260 --> 00:02:41.880
protecting San Francisco from his violent
fellow aliens.

00:02:42.920 --> 00:02:44.060
The Life Foundation

00:02:45.080 --> 00:02:49.980
In the comics, the shady organization of survivalists
called The Life Foundation was formed during

00:02:49.980 --> 00:02:53.920
the Cold War by people who believed that the
world was headed for a nuclear holocaust.

00:02:54.150 --> 00:02:58.860
To ensure the survival of civilized life on
Earth, the foundation built a massive fallout

00:02:58.879 --> 00:03:03.859
shelter for survivors or, at least, for those
wealthy enough to buy themselves a space.

00:03:03.859 --> 00:03:07.819
To protect the shelter and its inhabitants,
the Life Foundation kidnapped Venom and used

00:03:07.819 --> 00:03:13.700
him to create five new symbiotes: Agony, Phage,
Lasher, Scream, and Riot, all of which get

00:03:13.700 --> 00:03:15.640
bonded to Life Foundation employees.

00:03:15.760 --> 00:03:20.540
Reportedly, the big bad in Venom will be the
formidable Riot, latching himself primarily

00:03:20.540 --> 00:03:24.020
onto Riz Ahmed's character Life Foundation
boss Carlton Drake.

00:03:25.040 --> 00:03:26.240
Dreams of Carnage

00:03:26.940 --> 00:03:32.319
Venom's most famous symbiote nemesis is Carnage,
an unhinged offshoot of Venom that latches

00:03:32.320 --> 00:03:34.629
onto the psychotic killer Cletus Kasady.

00:03:34.780 --> 00:03:38.780
Though Sony has tried to keep his involvement
a secret, Kasady is reportedly being played

00:03:38.780 --> 00:03:43.129
in the film by Woody Harrelson though that
doesn't necessarily mean that Carnage himself

00:03:43.129 --> 00:03:43.880
is showing up.

00:03:44.060 --> 00:03:48.580
Speaking to Collider, Harrelson said his appearance
in Venom is more of a setup for the future.

00:03:48.580 --> 00:03:53.069
While the actor did not confirm he was playing
Kasady, he hinted at the tease - nature of

00:03:53.069 --> 00:03:54.069
his role, saying,

00:03:54.069 --> 00:03:57.240
"I'm in a little fraction of this movie, but
I'll be in the next one, you know?"

00:03:58.200 --> 00:03:58.700
She-Venom

00:03:59.600 --> 00:04:04.400
Michelle Williams stars in Venom as Anne Weying,
who in the comics is a hotshot lawyer and

00:04:04.400 --> 00:04:05.640
Eddie Brock's ex-wife.

00:04:05.930 --> 00:04:10.000
The movie makes tweaks to their relationship,
making her Brock's current girlfriend as opposed

00:04:10.000 --> 00:04:10.500
to his ex.

00:04:10.620 --> 00:04:14.980
Much like Cletus Kasady, Anne is another character
with some secret significance.

00:04:15.200 --> 00:04:19.660
In the comics, Weying becomes the character
She-Venom, gaining the powers of the symbiote

00:04:19.660 --> 00:04:21.160
after Brock allows the two to bond.

00:04:21.470 --> 00:04:25.700
Williams has already made it clear that the
movie Anne will be no pushover, but will she

00:04:25.710 --> 00:04:27.630
really transform into She-Venom?

00:04:27.630 --> 00:04:28.800
Probably not in this one.

00:04:28.980 --> 00:04:31.640
More likely, she's in the movie as a seed
of what could come.

00:04:31.780 --> 00:04:36.400
If Venom does well enough to warrant its own
sequels, you can bet we'll see more famous

00:04:36.410 --> 00:04:38.210
symbiotes taking turns in the spotlight.

